WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.500
 

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.280
What happens when you press a key on the keyboard? So if I'm sitting at Word or something and I type in something like "hello computerphile"

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:14.660
What actually happens between me hitting the 'h' of the keyboard and the computer displaying the 'h' on screen?

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:24.340
So we're looking at: How does the actual key press go from me physically pressing the key to the computer painting the letter on the screen,

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:31.960
and the same applies whether we are typing into a word processor or whether we are playing a game or anything that's requiring user input.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:39.440
There's a chain which starts off with the hardware, where we have a key that we're pressing

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.160
that's my finger there

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:45.260
and then we're gonna end up in the software

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.480
with the computer displaying the 'h' on screen.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.620
How does that actually happen? What's going on between these two things? Well, I mean...

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:59.320
The first place to start is "How does the system actually work?", and in many ways it's no different from your light switch.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:05.980
I mean, what the switch is there is, you effectively got an electrical circuit and you've got a switch

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:12.180
and when we press the button on the thing, we close that circuit, and then the current can pass through and we can get an electrical signal.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:19.600
Typically, in a computer system of the mid-80s for example, that'd be +5V, these days it's 4V, +3.3...

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.420
It doesn't matter; there's a certain voltage which signals that key as being pressed.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.120
How many different possibilities are there from a keyboard?

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:33.520
Obviously you got all the keys, and then you've got Shift and all the keys, you got Alt and all the keys... you know, there are all sorts of possibilites

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.260
Do you know how many there are in total?

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.320
Err, probably... Well, I mean... what was it?

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:45.460
I think it's a [PCY3?] keyboard, is the standard, so... Let's say there's 100 keys on a keyboard, roughly, if we think about... you got 26 letters of the alphabet

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.580
Ten numbers, numeric keypad, that's got another

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:50.720
One, tw...

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.940
Sixteen or so on it

00:01:53.380 --> 00:02:02.060
All the other keys... I think you're looking roughly, when you count more function keys, Help, Home, Clear, PgUp, PgDown, Shift, Left shift, Right shift...

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.360
Control, Alt, Delete...

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:09.340
Theoretically, there's probably 2¹⁰⁰ different possible combinations of keys you could press

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:15.300
from none of them being pressed to all of them being pressed, and all the combinations inbetween.

